    "thm-qc-tqc-ribbon-link-invariant": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qc-tqc-ribbon-modular-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **unitary ribbon fusion category** is a unitary fusion category with a\nunitary braiding and a ribbon structure in the sense of\n[[def-twist-and-ribbon-structure]]. Thus the twist $\\theta$ is a natural\nautomorphism of the identity satisfying the balancing identity and the\ndual-compatibility condition of that root definition, and each component is\nunitary: $\\theta_X^\\dagger=\\theta_X^{-1}$. A ribbon structure is additional\ndata; it is not supplied by fusion rules or by a braid-group representation\nalone.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-ribbon-evaluation-is-an-invariant-of-framed-colored-links",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $\\mathcal C$ be a\nribbon category and $X\\in\\mathcal C$. If $\\beta\\in B_n$\nand $\\beta'\\in B_{n'}$, with $n,n'\\ge1$, are braids whose blackboard-framed $X$-colored closures\nare isotopic as framed oriented tangles, then\n\n$$t_n(\\beta)=t_{n'}(\\beta')$$\n\nin $\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathcal C}(\\mathbf 1)$. More generally, $t$ is an\ninvariant of framed $X$-colored links: the value $t_n(\\beta)$ depends only on\nthe framed isotopy class of the closure of $\\beta$. Ordinary Markov\nstabilization is not a framed isotopy: it inserts a curl and is handled only\nafter writhe normalization.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-ribbon-evaluation-of-an-x-colored-closed-braid",
          "source_section": "Definition",
