          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qc-tqc-unitary-fusion-category",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal C$ be a fusion category over $\\mathbb C$ in the sense of\n[[def-fusion-and-multifusion-category]]. A **dagger structure** assigns to every\nmorphism $f:X\\to Y$ a morphism $f^\\dagger:Y\\to X$, conjugate-linearly, with\n$(g\\circ f)^\\dagger=f^\\dagger\\circ g^\\dagger$ and\n$(f^\\dagger)^\\dagger=f$. A **unitary fusion category** is such a fusion\ncategory equipped with this dagger so that every morphism space is a\nfinite-dimensional normed space, composition is submultiplicative,\n$\\|f^\\dagger\\|=\\|f\\|$, and $\\|f^\\dagger f\\|=\\|f\\|^2$. Thus each\n$\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathcal C}(X)$ is a finite-dimensional $C^*$-algebra.\nPositive endomorphisms are the elements of its usual $C^*$-positive cone\n(equivalently $u^\\dagger u$ for an endomorphism $u$); in particular, if $c$ is\nsimple and $\\operatorname{End}(c)=\\mathbb C\\operatorname{id}_c$, then\n$\\lambda\\operatorname{id}_c$ is positive exactly when $\\lambda\\ge0$. For any\nmorphism $f:X\\to Y$, $f^\\dagger f$ is positive in $\\operatorname{End}(X)$;\nalso require $f^\\dagger f=0$ to imply $f=0$. Semisimple direct-sum\ndecompositions can be chosen orthogonally:\ntheir inclusions $i_j$ satisfy $i_j^\\dagger i_k=0$ for $j\\ne k$,\n$i_j^\\dagger i_j=\\operatorname{id}$, and $\\sum_j i_j i_j^\\dagger=\\operatorname{id}$.\nTensor product preserves daggers:\n$(f\\otimes g)^\\dagger=f^\\dagger\\otimes g^\\dagger$. Its associator and unit\nconstraints are **unitary**, meaning $\\alpha^\\dagger=\\alpha^{-1}$,\n$\\lambda^\\dagger=\\lambda^{-1}$, and $\\rho^\\dagger=\\rho^{-1}$.\n\nThe underlying fusion category is rigid, so its evaluation and coevaluation\nmorphisms satisfy the duality zig-zag identities. Those morphisms are not\nrequired to be unitary isomorphisms. A braiding is additional structure; when\nit is specified as a **unitary braiding**, each component satisfies\n$c_{X,Y}^\\dagger=c_{X,Y}^{-1}$. Positivity and unitarity are extra structure,\nnot consequences of the fusion rules or of the braid relations alone.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-qc-tqc-fusion-tree-basis",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal C$ be a unitary fusion category and fix a set\n$\\operatorname{Irr}(\\mathcal C)$ of representatives for the simple\nisomorphism classes, with the tensor unit $\\mathbf1$ itself representing its\nclass. Throughout this definition, the ordered input charges\n$a_1,\\ldots,a_n$, total charge $d$, and all internal edge charges are labels\nin $\\operatorname{Irr}(\\mathcal C)$. Also fix a parenthesized binary fusion\ntree. At each internal vertex with incoming charges $x,y$ and outgoing charge\n$z$, choose one vector from an orthonormal basis of $V_{xy}^z$. An\n**admissible labeling** assigns a representative charge to each internal edge\nso that each chosen vertex basis is nonempty; a separate multiplicity-basis\nindex is retained at every vertex. Compose the vertex morphisms, inserting the\nassociators required by the chosen tree, to obtain a morphism from the\nparenthesized input tensor to $d$. These composite morphisms are the\n**fusion-tree vectors**. The family is called a **fusion-tree basis** when it\nis an orthonormal basis of the fixed-input, fixed-total fusion space. For the\nempty input list the tensor is $\\mathbf1$ and its empty tree vector is\n$\\operatorname{id}_{\\mathbf1}$ when $d=\\mathbf1$; for one input $a_1$, the\nonly tree vector is $\\operatorname{id}_{a_1}$ when $d=a_1$. The next local\nlemma proves the basis property for every finite input list and includes the\ndimension recursion ([[lem-qc-tqc-fusion-tree-dimension]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-qc-tqc-fusion-multiplicity-space-is-hilbert",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a,b,c$ be simple objects in a unitary fusion category. The pairing on\n$V_{ab}^c=\\operatorname{Hom}(a\\otimes b,c)$ defined by\n$f\\circ g^\\dagger=\\langle f,g\\rangle\\operatorname{id}_c$ is a\nfinite-dimensional positive-definite Hermitian inner product, linear in the\nfirst variable and conjugate-linear in the second.",
