          "quote": "Let $n\\ge0$ be an integer, with $S_0=\\{1\\}$ as in\n[[def-partition-young-diagram-and-conjugate-partition]]. Let $A:=\\mathbb Z[v^{\\pm1}]$ be the ring of Laurent polynomials in one\nindeterminate $v$ over $\\mathbb Z$. For $n\\ge2$ the **generic type-A Hecke\nalgebra** $H_v(n)$ is the associative unital $A$-algebra presented by generators\n$T_1,\\dots,T_{n-1}$ and the relations\n$$T_i^2=(v-1)T_i+v\\quad(1\\le i<n),\\qquad T_iT_{i+1}T_i=T_{i+1}T_iT_{i+1}\\quad(1\\le i\\le n-2),\\qquad T_iT_j=T_jT_i\\quad(1\\le i,j<n,\\ |i-j|>1),$$\nthat is, the quotient of the free unital associative $A$-algebra on\n$T_1,\\dots,T_{n-1}$ by the two-sided ideal generated by these relators. The\nfirst relation is written in the **RG-13 quadratic normalization**\n$(T_i-v)(T_i+1)=0$, equivalent to the displayed form over $A$. For $n\\le1$ there\nare no generators and we set $H_v(n):=A$.\n\n**Standard basis elements.** Let $S_n=\\operatorname{Sym}(\\{1,\\dots,n\\})$ with\nsimple transpositions $s_i=(i\\ i+1)$\n([[def-symmetric-group]]), and let $w\\in S_n$ have inversion length $\\ell(w)=\\ell$\n([[def-weyl-group-and-length-for-finite-gl-n]]). Choose a reduced expression\n$w=s_{i_1}\\cdots s_{i_\\ell}$, that is, a word of the minimal length $\\ell(w)$\nrepresenting $w$, and put\n$$T_w:=T_{i_1}\\cdots T_{i_\\ell}\\in H_v(n),$$\nthe empty product when $w$ is the identity of $S_n$, so that $T_w=1$ for that\nelement. That $T_w$ is independent of the chosen reduced expression, and that\nthe elements $T_w$ form an $A$-basis of $H_v(n)$, is proved in the\nstandard-basis theorem below\n([[thm-standard-basis-of-the-generic-type-a-hecke-algebra]]); the notation\ndistinguishes the generator $T_i$ with $1\\le i\\le n-1$ from $T_w$ for the group\nelement $w$.\n\n**Specializations.** Let $R$ be a commutative $A$-algebra with structure map\n$\\mathbb Z[v^{\\pm1}]\\to R$, $v\\mapsto v_0$, and suppose $v_0\\in R^\\times$. The\n**specialization** of $H_v(n)$ at $v_0$ is the $R$-algebra\n$R\\otimes_AH_v(n)$; it is presented over $R$ by the images of $T_1,\\dots,T_{n-1}$\nsubject to the same relations with $v$ replaced by $v_0$, and we write $H_{v_0}(n)$\nfor it. Two specializations are used on this page: $v\\mapsto1$, where the\nquadratic relation becomes $T_i^2=1$ together with the braid and commutation\nrelations, and $v\\mapsto q$, where $q$ is the prime power defining\n$\\operatorname{GL}_n(\\mathbb F_q)$; the latter specializes the generic algebra to\nthe Hecke algebra attached to the finite general linear group. The condition\n$v_0\\in R^\\times$ is part of the definition of a specialization and, for a nonzero coefficient ring $R$, excludes $v_0=0$. The zero ring\nis allowed: its unique element is a unit and its specialization is the zero algebra.",
